        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "How Often to Seal Marble",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-often-to-seal-marble/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should marble countertops be sealed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Test them every three to six months and reseal the areas that absorb water. On a busy kitchen counter that often means the strip near the sink and cooktop every six to twelve months, with the rest of the counter lasting longer. Lightly used marble counters may go several years between applications."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does marble sealer last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the product, the stone and the use. Manufacturers typically quote one to five years or more for impregnating sealers, with premium solvent and fluoropolymer products at the upper end. Spray-on maintenance sealers last months rather than years. The water test is the only reliable way to tell on your own stone."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do marble floors need sealing as often as countertops?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually less often, because floors see water and grit rather than oil and acids. Walkways and areas around tubs and sinks wear first. Test those spots every six months, and expect to reseal floors every one to three years with a good impregnating sealer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you over-seal marble?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Applying sealer to stone that is still repelling water leaves excess product on the surface, which dries as haze, streaks or sticky patches. The stone itself is not harmed, but the residue has to be removed. Only seal areas that fail the water test, and always wipe off the excess."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does polished marble need sealing less often than honed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Generally, yes. Polishing closes the surface, so polished marble absorbs more slowly and holds sealer longer than honed or tumbled marble. Honed finishes have more open pores and tend to need resealing more often, though they hide etching much better."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I reseal after cleaning off a stain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. A poultice draws out sealer along with the stain, so the treated patch becomes more absorbent than its surroundings. Let it dry for at least a day, then reseal the area and blend the coat a few inches into the stone around it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it bad to never reseal marble?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Marble will not deteriorate structurally without sealer, but it becomes progressively easier to stain. On light marble kitchens that can mean permanent oil shadows. Very dense polished marble may rarely need sealing, which is why testing is better than either never sealing or sealing by habit."
          }
        }
      ],
