    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          There is no fixed schedule for sealing marble, and the people who quote one are guessing on your behalf. Test the stone every six months with a little water, reseal the areas that absorb, and leave alone the areas that do not. In practice that means busy kitchen counters may need attention once or twice a year, while a guest bathroom vanity sealed with a good impregnator can go years.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Test Twice a Year, Reseal When It Absorbs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Test sealed marble roughly every six months and reseal only when the test says so. Pour about a quarter cup of water on a clean, dry, well-used spot, wait fifteen minutes, then wipe it off. If the stone underneath has darkened, water got in and the area needs resealing. If there is no mark, the protection is intact and adding more sealer would only leave residue on top.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is the same approach we recommend for granite in <a href="/how-often-to-seal-granite-countertops/">how often to seal granite countertops</a>, and it matters even more on marble. Marble is softer and more absorbent than most granite, and a stain on light Carrara or Calacatta is far more visible than the same mark on a speckled dark granite. A short, regular test catches fading protection before a spill turns into a permanent shadow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test more than one spot. The area beside a kitchen sink is scrubbed and wetted many times a day, so it loses protection first, while the far end of an island may stay water-repellent for years. On floors, test the walkway between rooms and the spot in front of the sink or tub rather than under a rug. It is entirely normal for one part of a surface to need resealing while the rest does not.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough guide, not a rule</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Water test duration</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">6 months</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">How often to test</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">6-12 mo</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical busy kitchen reseal</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-5 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical quiet vanity reseal</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those ranges are typical rather than guaranteed, and they assume a decent impregnating sealer applied properly. A spray-on maintenance product may need topping up every few months, while a premium solvent impregnator on a lightly used surface can go much longer. The <a href="/best-marble-sealer/">marble sealer roundup</a> lists the expected life of each product we recommend.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-varies" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why There Is No Single Interval
          </h2>

          <p>
            Marble is a natural stone, and its porosity varies from slab to slab and even across a single slab. Dense, polished marble can absorb water so slowly that it barely needs sealing, while a honed or more open stone can soak up a spill within a minute. The same sealer applied to both will last very different lengths of time, simply because the stones themselves behave differently.
          </p>

          <p>
            The finish matters too. Polishing closes the surface, so polished marble generally absorbs less and holds sealer longer than honed marble. Honed, brushed and tumbled finishes leave more open pores and more surface area, which means they take more sealer, absorb spills faster and usually need resealing more often. That is a fair trade for anyone who chose honed marble because it hides etching better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is use. A kitchen countertop sees oil, acids, heat, scrubbing and constant cleaning, all of which slowly wear sealer out of the top of the pores. A bathroom vanity sees toothpaste, soap and occasional splashes. A fireplace surround sees almost nothing. Two pieces of identical stone sealed on the same day can therefore need retreating years apart.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, the product and the quality of the application set the starting point. A thorough application that soaks in fully, as described in our guide to <a href="/how-to-seal-marble/">how to seal marble</a>, lasts longer than a rushed coat on damp stone. That is why a calendar reminder to test is useful but a calendar reminder to reseal is not: the test reflects all of these variables at once.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-room" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Typical Intervals by Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            The table below gives typical ranges for how often marble in different places ends up needing a fresh coat, assuming a good impregnating sealer and sensible cleaning. Treat them as a starting point for how often to test, not a reason to skip testing. If your water test says the stone is still repelling, it does not need sealing yet, whatever the table suggests.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Location</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical reseal interval</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Test every</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kitchen countertop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-12 months near sink and cooktop; longer elsewhere</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-6 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Oil and constant cleaning wear sealer fastest</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bathroom vanity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Watch around the faucet and soap dish</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shower walls and floor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Daily water and soap; seal grout too</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Interior floors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mopping and grit wear the walkways</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tabletops and furniture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rings from glasses are usually etches</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fireplace surrounds</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years or more</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yearly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low exposure; soot is the main risk</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Exterior marble</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Weather, freeze-thaw and UV; use a product rated for exterior use</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Kitchens are the outlier. Even with a premium sealer, the strip beside the sink often loses protection within a year because of hot water, dish soap and scrubbing. Many owners simply reseal that strip and the area around the cooktop once or twice a year and leave the rest of the counter alone, which is the efficient approach and avoids building up residue on areas that do not need it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Showers deserve attention for a different reason. Grout is usually more absorbent than the marble around it, and water constantly working into grout joints can carry soap scum and minerals with it. Sealing the grout at the same time as the stone, with a product rated for both or a dedicated product from our <a href="/best-grout-sealer/">grout sealer guide</a>, keeps the whole surface consistent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-sealer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much the Sealer Itself Changes the Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Products vary widely in how long they last. Premium solvent-based impregnators and fluoropolymer formulas are designed for multi-year protection on stone, while light spray-and-wipe sealers are intended for frequent top-ups. The table in our <a href="/best-marble-sealer/">best marble sealers</a> guide puts typical reapplication at one to three years for budget solvent products, three to five years for premium ones, and longer for the most expensive formulations.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those figures come from manufacturers' claims and typical experience, and they assume moderate use. On a busy kitchen counter, expect the low end of any range or less. On a vanity or tabletop, the high end is realistic. If you have been resealing every few months and the stone keeps absorbing quickly, the product may not suit that stone, or the application may not be soaking in, which is worth fixing before buying more of the same.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spray-on maintenance sealers have a role, but it is a supporting one. They are useful for topping up the high-wear strip near a sink between full applications, and they are easy to use. They do not replace a proper impregnating coat on stone that is genuinely absorbing, and using them as the only sealer usually means sealing several times a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Enhancing sealers follow the same logic: their protective life is set by the impregnating ingredients, not by the color effect. Once applied, the enhanced look generally lasts, but the stain protection still wears and still needs testing. If you top up an enhanced surface, use the same product so the color stays consistent, and test on a small area first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wear" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Wears Sealer Out Faster
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cleaning products are the biggest factor most people control. Acidic cleaners such as vinegar sprays and many bathroom products etch marble directly, and strongly alkaline products such as bleach, ammonia-based glass cleaners and degreasers break down sealer over time. Using a pH-neutral stone cleaner, as described in our guide to <a href="/how-to-clean-marble-countertops/">cleaning marble countertops</a>, can noticeably extend the time between reseals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Abrasion is the next. Scouring powders, abrasive pads and melamine sponges scratch polished marble and grind away the top layer of sealed stone. Grit carried in on shoes does the same to floors, which is why walkways lose protection before the edges of a room. Doormats, felt pads under furniture and a soft dust mop all help floors hold their sealer longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Standing water and heat also play a part. Water pooling around a faucet for hours every day slowly works into the stone, and hot pans or appliances placed directly on marble can degrade sealer locally. Trivets, a dish rack with a drip tray, and wiping the faucet area dry once a day all reduce wear on the most vulnerable spots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stain removal resets the clock in that spot. A poultice uses solvents or peroxide to draw a stain out of the stone, and in doing so it removes sealer from that area too. After any poultice treatment, let the stone dry fully and reseal the treated patch, feathering the sealer a few inches into the surrounding stone. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-stains-from-marble/">removing stains from marble</a> covers that step.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Signs It Is Time, Besides the Water Test
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water that used to bead and now spreads flat is the earliest sign. Freshly sealed marble usually beads water into tight drops; as the sealer wears, drops flatten and then start soaking in. You will often notice this while wiping the counter, well before your next scheduled test, especially around the sink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spills leaving darker shadows that take a while to fade is the next. A quick splash of water that leaves a dark patch for several minutes after wiping shows that the stone is absorbing. It is not yet a stain, because water evaporates, but oil or wine on the same spot would be. Treat it as the prompt to reseal that area.
          </p>

          <p>
            New stains appearing where none appeared before are the late sign. If oil marks start showing up beside the cooktop or coffee rings near the kettle, protection has already worn thin. Remove the stain first, then reseal. Sealing over it would trap it in the stone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dull spots, by contrast, are not a sign of failing sealer. A dull, whitish ring or mark with no color is almost always an etch from acid, and sealer was never going to stop it. Resealing will not help; polishing will, as described in our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-etching-from-marble/">removing etching from marble</a>. Knowing the difference saves both money and frustration.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spot" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Spot Resealing vs a Full Reseal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most of the time, the right answer is to reseal only the areas that fail the water test. Clean the area thoroughly, let it dry for at least a day, then flood, dwell and wipe as you would for a full application, feathering the edges a few inches into the surrounding stone so there is no visible boundary. A properly applied impregnating sealer leaves no surface film, so there is no line to see.
          </p>

          <p>
            A full reseal makes sense when the water test fails in most places, when you are moving into a home and have no idea what was used before, or after a professional honing or polishing, which removes the top layer of stone and the sealer with it. In those cases, treat the stone as if it were new and follow the full process from cleaning through curing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid the temptation to reseal the whole surface just to be safe. Areas that are still repelling cannot absorb more product, so the excess sits on top and dries as haze or sticky patches. Over-sealing is one of the most common causes of cloudy marble, and it is entirely avoidable by testing before you apply.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a simple record of when and where you sealed and with what. After a year or two, you will know exactly how long protection lasts on each part of your marble, and you can test only when it is likely to be needed. That record turns a guessing game into a routine that takes a few minutes twice a year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resealing on a fixed calendar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealing every six months whether the stone needs it or not leads to residue build-up on areas that are still protected. Test on a schedule instead, and reseal only where the water test shows absorption. That keeps the surface clear and saves product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing only one spot</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Marble wears unevenly, and the area beside the sink fails long before the rest. A single test in a quiet corner gives false reassurance. Test the busiest areas first, and test at least three places on a counter or along a floor walkway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming sealer for etching</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dull, colorless marks from acids are etches, and no impregnating sealer prevents them. Resealing every time an etch appears wastes time and product. Polish the etch, and keep resealing tied to the water test rather than to how the surface looks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using harsh cleaners between reseals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bleach, ammonia, vinegar and abrasive powders all shorten the life of sealer or damage the stone directly. Switching to a pH-neutral stone cleaner is often the single most effective way to stretch the time between applications.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting to reseal after a poultice</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Poultice treatments remove sealer along with the stain, leaving a patch more absorbent than the stone around it. Once the treated area is completely dry, reseal it and feather the coat into the surrounding surface.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should marble countertops be sealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Test them every three to six months and reseal the areas that absorb water. On a busy kitchen counter that often means the strip near the sink and cooktop every six to twelve months, with the rest of the counter lasting longer. Lightly used marble counters may go several years between applications.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does marble sealer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the product, the stone and the use. Manufacturers typically quote one to five years or more for impregnating sealers, with premium solvent and fluoropolymer products at the upper end. Spray-on maintenance sealers last months rather than years. The water test is the only reliable way to tell on your own stone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do marble floors need sealing as often as countertops?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually less often, because floors see water and grit rather than oil and acids. Walkways and areas around tubs and sinks wear first. Test those spots every six months, and expect to reseal floors every one to three years with a good impregnating sealer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you over-seal marble?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Applying sealer to stone that is still repelling water leaves excess product on the surface, which dries as haze, streaks or sticky patches. The stone itself is not harmed, but the residue has to be removed. Only seal areas that fail the water test, and always wipe off the excess.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does polished marble need sealing less often than honed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally, yes. Polishing closes the surface, so polished marble absorbs more slowly and holds sealer longer than honed or tumbled marble. Honed finishes have more open pores and tend to need resealing more often, though they hide etching much better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I reseal after cleaning off a stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A poultice draws out sealer along with the stain, so the treated patch becomes more absorbent than its surroundings. Let it dry for at least a day, then reseal the area and blend the coat a few inches into the stone around it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to never reseal marble?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Marble will not deteriorate structurally without sealer, but it becomes progressively easier to stain. On light marble kitchens that can mean permanent oil shadows. Very dense polished marble may rarely need sealing, which is why testing is better than either never sealing or sealing by habit.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

